WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Hank: Good morning John!

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.399
I am still excited about Pizzamas; this is
all very exciting except for your mustache,

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:06.899
which is not exciting at all.

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:09.420
But you know who I'm not as excited as?

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.420
This guy:

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.420
Train Guy: Oh my god!

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.420
WAAAAA!

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.450
Hank: I consider myself to be something of
an expert in the genre’s of viral videos.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.500
There's, there's lots of them: there's epic
cutes, and epic wins, epic fails, epic weirds.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.619
But for me, as a viewer, the most pure and
rewarding kind of viral video is the epic

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:26.619
excitement.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:29.820
Beautiful, unabashed, uncontrolled excitement.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.880
It's such a wonderful feeling that just watching
other people have it makes me feel wonderful.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.550
And John, today, I want to be stupid excited
about the very idea of excitement.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:40.550
Meta!

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.950
We're gonna start out with Rachael Nedrow,
whose voice you may have heard in a couple

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.170
of Skrillex songs.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.170
Among many other things in what I'm sure is
a full and complete life, Rachael has competitively

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.170
stacked cups.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.600
I'm not sure how difficult this is, I'm not
sure amazing it is, it certainly looks amazing

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.600
and impressive.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.800
But her reaction is the thing I'm here for:

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.800
Rachael: Yes!

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.800
Oh my gosh!

00:00:58.800 --> 00:00:59.800
Oh my gosh!

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:00.880
Oh my gosh!

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.880
Hank: Auhhh!

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.180
Now let's look at the video we started this
video with, this guy loves trains:

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.130
Train Guy: I've been waiting for this moment
for months and it's finally here.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.620
Look at that, a 1953 EA.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:15.620
Woohoo!

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.310
(train horn) Oh my god!

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.960
(screaming) Listen to that horn!

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.020
That horn!

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.820
Next, we have Aleksander Gamme, who is hiking
across the Antarctic.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.171
When you're doing this, so you don't have
to carry your supplies the whole way, you

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:30.630
leave some of your supplies in caches along
the way, so you can pick them up on the way

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.630
back.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.230
And in his final cache, after surviving on
what I assume is not the tastiest food in

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.530
the world, he discovers that he has left some
treats.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.270
Aleksander: Yah!

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.270
Yaah!

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.270
Yaaah!

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:47.750
Hank: I mean, he put those cheese doodles
there didn't, he know what to expect?

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:52.800
I guess he forgot and it was just the most
surprising bag of cheese doodles of all time!

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.729
And surprise seems to be a part of it, not
knowing what the outcome is and then finding

00:01:56.729 --> 00:01:58.380
indeed that there is more.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.920
For example, there was another historic engine
on the back of that other engine and the guy

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.409
got even more excited:

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:05.240
Train Guy: Oh no!

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.060
It's a BL2 too!

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.060
Ah!

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.060
Ah!

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.060
Ah!

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.060
The SNC 52!

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.060
Hank: Sometimes it's amazing to see people
get excited about things that you just don't

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.060
understand why they're so excited but yes,
yes, I feel it too!

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.700
Other times you're right there with that person,
like I'm willing to bet there are a lot of

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.260
people who wish they might be able to like
grow up and become Luke Skywalker, but actually

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.520
becoming a hero in the Star Wars saga not
something that happens to very many people.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.400
But thanks to some foresight, and the internet,
we get to be there with John Boyega as he

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.819
sees the Star Wars teaser trailer and the
part that he plays in it:

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:39.090
John: "Woah oh oh!

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.090
Oh oh oh!

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.090
What?!

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.090
What?!"

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.090
Hank: Yess!

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.780
Finally, this list would not be complete without
the most famous internet excited guy of all

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.340
time, Yosemitebear, as he comes across a double
rainbow that had become, through the magic

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.490
of the internet and the work of the Gregory
brothers, legendary.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.042
Yosemitebear: Double rainbow all the way across
the sky!

00:02:58.042 --> 00:02:59.550
It's so intense.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.070
Hank: Sometimes it feels like the only moment
when it's culturally acceptable to feel that

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.270
kind of uncontrolled excitement is in sporting
events.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.300
And I'm not really into sports.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.350
For me, the last time I felt like that level
of excitement and just couldn't control myself

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.910
was in 2012 when the Curiosity Rover definitely
was safe on the surface of Mars.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.340
What I realized though, watching these clips,
it that there is so much to be excited about.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.580
These people are open to the beauty and amazingness
of nature and of human accomplishment, and

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.610
of their own accomplishments.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.720
And they're kind of my heroes.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.160
I wanna be more like them.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.629
It's kind of funny, but I feel like it requires
work, to say to myself 'Don't shut it down,

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:37.910
Love things, by yourself or with others and
without fear.'

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:42.750
These videos for me are a master class in
being open to the wondrousness of nature and

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.060
of the human condition and of the self.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.010
So I put them in a playlist in the description
below, you can click on it and watch all of

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.000
them, and it's just wonderful thing to do.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.430
John, I'll see you tomorrow.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:52.470
I'm freaking my dog out.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.470
"Why are you screaming, dad?"

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.660
I need to make a video, and I'm going to scream,
during it, so just lay down and just assume

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.690
that I'm doing human things that are weird.

